SM(485,{"n":"Acts 26","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 26.mp3","d":2508.8,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.32,"Then Agrippa said to Paul, you are permitted to speak for yourself."],[27.6,"So Paul stretched out his hand and answered for himself."],[32.8,"So it's an odd turn of phrase, but St."],[37.6,"Paul apparently spoke with his hands."],[41.92,"Despite not being the Italian in the room."],[44.24,"Well politically incorrect."],[48.0,"Yeah. Um. And he answers."],[55.12,"I think myself happy, King Agrippa, because today I shall answer for myself before you concerning all the things of which I am accused by the Jews, especially because you are expert in all customs and questions which have to do with the Jews."],[71.64,"Therefore I beg you to hear me patiently."],[95.64,"But they didn't have television, radio, internet."],[101.8,"Most people were not literate and couldn't afford books."],[104.8,"Um so hearing public orations and on topics and debates and these sort of things, it wasn't just sermons at the synagogue."],[115.6,"This was part of everyone's daily life."],[119.22,"Going to hear educated people speak on topics or debate and discuss topics."],[125.06,"Uh we talked about this especially in Athens when St."],[127.94,"Paul was there, you know, where he goes to sort of participate in these constant ongoing discussions in the in the marketplace."],[135.62,"But so St."],[137.06,"Paul, we know, is not only educated in the scriptures with rabbinical training, but we know he also had what was at the time a classical Roman education."],[146.82,"Uh we'll see some places in his epistles."],[149.06,"He quotes Stoic philosophers"],[153.34,"He quotes, so he has this not just not only does he know have this command of the Hebrew scriptures and of Jewish traditions, but he's also versed in sort of classical learning."],[167.66,"And part of that was learning to speak."],[169.5,"Part of that was learning methods of rhetoric."],[173.26,"And so, well, to us it seems like sucking up."],[176.62,"This is the way you would begin a speech by flattering your audience."],[182.16,"So by talking about the worthiness of your audience, you know, because rhetoric like this was seen as creating sort of a relationship between the person and the audience."],[193.05,"So you start out by complimenting them."],[193.21,"you You're hoping they're going to be complimenting by the end of it."],[199.09,"So that's part of the give and take."],[202.77,"And so he's kind of buttering him up."],[207.41,"He's also kind of pointing out that while King Agrippa was probably not an expert in everything pertaining to the Jews."],[218.45,"He, as opposed to Festus, and as opposed to Felix, although Felix was married to a Jewish woman, as opposed to Festus, especially, he does understand Jewish culture and that kind of thing more than the other people he's been sort of on trial before."],[237.17,"So he's kind of of indicating, okay, Agrippa, I'm I could speak to you on a different level and in a different way than some these other people I've been speaking to, because you'll get what I'm saying and where I'm coming from in a way that they don't."],[263.21,"All the Jews know."],[265.45,"They knew me from the first, if they were willing to testify that according to the strictest sect of our religion, I lived a Pharisee."],[276.81,"So, first of all, he's saying, I'm not just some stranger who wandered into Jerusalem and caused trouble."],[282.89,"These people know full well who I am."],[283.37,"And remember, at the beginning of the book of Acts, he was working for them"],[289.33,"So they know exactly who he is."],[291.17,"He's not just some stranger."],[292.85,"And he says, and if you ask them, and they're honest, they'll say that I am a member of the strictest uh sect of the of the religion."],[307.51,"And that that's how I conducted my life, as a as a Pharisee."],[312.01,"And we so we've seen all through the Gospels and in Acts that the Pharisees, uh, one thing you could definitely say about them is that they were strict."],[320.97,"That they have uh not only we talked about how they they believed in building a hedge around the law."],[328.02,"So if uh, for example, taking the name of the Lord in vain is a sin, well, how do we make sure that we never sin?"],[336.34,"Well, we never say the name of the Lord."],[339.14,"We don't say it, we don't write it, we don't, and then we can never take it in vain because we never take it at all."],[345.27,"And we talked about the Sabbath rules."],[347.12,"You're not allowed to work on the Sabbath."],[348.48,"Well, what constitutes work?"],[350.08,"Okay, well, you can only walk so far, you can only you know, you can do this, you can't do that."],[356.54,"Detailed rules. You you cannot, today, you cannot tear toilet paper."],[362.2,"Yeah, Orthodox Judaism has kept a lot of that."],[364.28,"You can't flip a light switch because that's there's a spark and that's kindling a fire."],[369.1,"So there's all these detailed uh a lot of buildings in New York where where Jewish folks live have pneumatic elevators rather than electric."],[380.26,"For that reason."],[382.16,"So strict they got."],[383.69,"And we talked about how part of the problem between Christ and the Pharisees was always that the Pharisees, by having all of these extra rules to try to keep people away from sin, started to confuse those extra rules with the sin."],[400.41,"So now it's not it's not a sin to work work on the Sabbath day, just."],[404.49,"It's if you walk more than a quarter of a mile, you've now violated the Sabbath and you're worthy of death."],[411.79,"And so they've confused their rule with the law."],[418.15,"And so that that causes sort of constant friction."],[421.19,"You know, Christ will heal someone on the Sabbath, and they're like, well, why didn't you come on another day and get healed?"],[428.71,"Because healing is work."],[430.15,"Jesus is saying, you know, says so, is it lawful to do good or to do evil on the Sabbath?"],[434.55,"So if I did something good, how is that unlawful?"],[438.07,"Because they've made those rules part of God's law, which are things that he never said."],[446.47,"But so, yeah, St."],[447.91,"Paul is, you know, Agrippa, even if he doesn't have a great firsthand knowledge of the Jews, he will know that the Pharisees are really strict."],[456.31,"He will know that much."],[457.43,"The Pharisees probably wouldn't go. anywhere near him, for example, because he's a Gentile"],[463.15,"The Pharisees generally hated the Herodians."],[467.47,"And now I stand and am judged for the hope of the promise made by God to our fathers."],[475.15,"To this promise are twelve tribes earnestly serving God night and day, hope to attain."],[481.87,"For this hope's sake, King Agrippa, I am accused by the Jews."],[486.19,"Why should it be thought incredible to you by you that God raises the dead?"],[493.63,"So St. Paul has taken a couple of things."],[496.91,"But remember, he's already done this once with the Sanhedrin, where he mentioned, I'm on trial here because I'm a Pharisee and I believe in the resurrection."],[503.79,"And the Sadducees and the Pharisees all got into it"],[508.63,"The Pharisees started decided to take his side because, you know, hey, yeah."],[513.19,"Because the Pharisees believed in the resurrection of the dead."],[516.95,"And we talked about how that, you know, they didn't believe in it really in the sense of Christ having risen from the dead."],[523.43,"That's not really what we're talking about."],[525.75,"They believed in the idea that on the last day God would visit the world and then there would be a resurrection of the dead."],[534.07,"And this was related to, you really see this coming out like in in uh 2 Maccabees in some of the books written late in the New Testament period, it becomes really clear."],[546.23,"Because the idea is that that. God has promised He's going to do justice"],[559.07,"But we could all look around at the world and see righteous people who are suffering, and wicked people who don't seem all that cursed."],[567.65,"Who seem to be doing okay."],[569.35,"Doing well. And so the resurrection, the hope of the resurrection is the answer to that."],[575.77,"Is the answer to that."],[576.73,"Is that, well, there's going to come a day when God's going to judge the world."],[581.22,"He's going to bring justice to the world."],[583.3,"And that justice is going to take the form of those people who were righteous who have been suffering are going to receive their reward."],[590.58,"And those people who are wicked, who have been prospering, sometimes at the expense of those righteous people, they're going to get their reward, which is going to be punishment."],[601.42,"Well, in Ezekiel and with the dry bones, isn't that at least pointing in the direction of the right?"],[608.46,"Yeah, it's something that develops through the whole testament."],[611.5,"You see it full blown, like in the book of wisdom and in 2 Maccabees, where it just straight out talks about it, literally."],[617.98,"You know, in 2 Maccabees, when the Maccabean martyrs are being tortured, literally tortured to death by the the Greek rulers for refusing to repudiate their God and and the Torah."],[632.82,"Uh they say, you know, I know that God will knit my body back together on the last day and I will live again."],[640.9,"You know, so it's very, you know, very, very, very literal and direct stated."],[647.46,"But yeah, it begins."],[648.66,"I mean, it's a I mean Job has references to it."],[653.06,"Yeah, so I mean it's it's it's uh all through it becomes increasingly clear."],[658.9,"The same way the idea of the Messiah."],[661.44,"It's there in Genesis 3, the seed of the woman."],[664.34,"But it becomes clearer and clearer as the old testament plays out until you get to the the birth of Christ."],[671.06,"Uh so these things kind of are revealed and come to be understood gradually."],[675.26,"The Jews believe that now."],[676.5,"I I I'm understanding this that a lot don't."],[679.14,"A lot don't."],[680.26,"A lot don't."],[681.3,"Yeah. You know, same thing's true in the Jewish community now"],[681.74,"So just like then, there were Sadducees who didn't believe in the resurrection."],[684.94,"There are Pharisees who did."],[688.62,"Some some Jews believe in an afterlife, some don't."],[692.7,"Isn't it the Orthodox who believe in the afterlife today?"],[696.86,"Mostly. Yeah. The Reformed."],[700.38,"Yeah. Yeah. Well, some of the Reformed don't even believe God exists."],[708.22,"They just believe in the Jewish traditions and way of life, kind of thing."],[713.34,"So they're even kind of further."],[717.98,"But there are even some Orthodox sects that don't."],[724.38,"But yeah, more Orthodox believe it in than the others."],[726.54,"Yeah, yeah Most of my Jewish friends growing up were conservatives, which is someplace in between."],[734.18,"Which is pieces of this and pieces of that."],[741.14,"But so this is this is what St."],[743.7,"Paul is saying, he's talking about the promise to our fathers."],[746.58,"For which the for this that's this the promises are made at the end of Deuteronomy about the blessings and the curses."],[753.48,"For for following the ways of God or not."],[756.78,"And so judgment, and this is how it's portrayed in the prophets in the Old Testament."],[761.34,"We always think of judgment in a negative sense."],[765.14,"And that's because, and by and large, we should, because we're sinners."],[770.06,"And but not only that, because uh we are much more often on the side of the well to do and the oppressor than on the side of the oppressed."],[781.42,"Um the promise is when God is talking in the Old Testament and He talks to orphans and widows, you know, these people who are impoverished, you know, to the point of you know, starving to death and this kind of thing."],[794.46,"When he's talking to them about vindicating them, that's not most of us."],[798.72,"Um it's some people, but it's not most of us, it's not."],[802.96,"And so uh, and this this is also part of the conflict between Christ and the Pharisees, is that the Pharisees are very certain that they're on the righteous side of that equation?"],[813.12,"' Were going're to going. to be blessed and you know, they enslave the Gentiles, those rotten Gentiles, and da-da-da-da-da"],[819.8,"And Jesus trying to get through to them, you know, you're not really on the side of the fence that you think you are."],[827.4,"You know, that that uh that's so part of what the parable of the Pharisee and the publican is about."],[832.46,"This tax collector who you hate."],[834.3,"If he's repentant, he's the one who's righteous, not you."],[838.96,"You know. Um, but so that this is the the hope he's talking about."],[844.08,"He's talking about the hope of the resurrection."],[846.66,"And so when he starts out here, Agrippa wouldn't have heard at least, oh, he's talking about whether that Jesus guy rose from the dead or not."],[854.81,"He would have just said, why, why is it so weird that God could raise the dead if he wanted to?"],[860.69,"Yeah. Right now he's just talking about the resurrection as the Pharisees understood it."],[866.39,"Yeah, yeah. He's trying to bring him on board."],[868.31,"Now he's gonna go somewhere else, but he's trying to bring him on board with at least the Pharisee level, of understanding of this."],[875.77,"And we we talked about when we talked about St."],[878.25,"Paul's conversion, that uh we tend to look at, we call it a conversion, for example."],[886.65,"We have this idea that, well, St."],[888.73,"Paul was a Jew and he was a Pharisee, and then he became a Christian."],[892.01,"Uh-huh Now that happened in 35 AD, which is three years after Jesus rose from the dead, at most, maybe only two."],[900.23,"Two two, three years"],[905.48,"There was not a religion, Judaism, and another religion, Christianity, two years after Jesus rose from the dead."],[914.22,"Where you could leave one and join the other."],[917.56,"There were at that point there were Jews who had accepted that Jesus was the Messiah."],[923.32,"And there were those that hadn't, either because they'd rejected the idea or they hadn't even heard it."],[929.56,"Did Paul ever like completely see a difference?"],[932.76,"I mean, but in the sense that yeah, he took, you know, open up the Gentiles, but he said, like, they are an all all Israel that are of Israel."],[941.46,"And basically, you know, the theology was a basically is that the church, the Israel is is the church, those who believe in Jesus, and these people have they chosen to leave uh the faith."],[953.42,"You know. And that's it."],[955.24,"There's a remnant."],[956.04,"There's a remnant of Israel."],[958.49,"Who has accepted Jesus."],[960.03,"Who has accepted Jesus, and these Gentiles are being grafted in."],[963.47,"That's what he's going to talk about in Romans."],[965.65,"And these people who haven't accepted Jesus as the Messiah have cut themselves off."],[970.77,"Yeah. Um, but so Saint St."],[973.57,"Paul is going to refer to himself as a Pharisee in in the present tense several times in his epistles."],[981.01,"So it's not that Saint Paul is on the road to Emmaus and encounters Jesus. and completely changes his religion"],[993.05,"His religious worldview."],[994.91,"He still believes the same things."],[997.71,"He still, I mean, he believed a Messiah was going to come before."],[1002.59,"Believes that a Messiah has come afterwards."],[1004.35,"Believes in the resurrection of the dead, believes."],[1007.13,"The Torah believes all these things are true."],[1009.53,"What changes is where he is in terms of the time frame of the redemption of the world."],[1015.85,"He goes from believing a Messiah is going to come to believing the Messiah has come."],[1021.77,"And this is what he's done."],[1024.89,"But from his perspective, he had not changed religion."],[1028.17,"So that's the point he keeps making in these defenses about the Jews."],[1032.21,"I haven't abandoned the Jewish faith, Jewish beliefs, the Torah."],[1036.46,"I haven't abandoned these things."],[1038.7,"In fact, he's ultimately going to argue, as you pointed out, in Robinson, they're the ones who have abandoned these things."],[1044.78,"I've stayed true to these things."],[1047.98,"And so, yeah, that's very much how he sees it."],[1051.82,"So he's not just, you know, for the sake of argument, let's just start with the Pharisee stuff."],[1056.96,"I mean, he believes there's going to be a resurrection of everyone on the last day."],[1060.0,"He still believes this."],[1062.48,"But so this is where he's going to start."],[1065.84,"He's going to start at that level that your common Jewish Pharisees."],[1070.0,"This is something Agrippa would have heard before, at least."],[1072.48,"This is an idea that he would have heard before."],[1074.24,"And so it represents a starting point"],[1080.28,"Indeed, I myself thought I must do many things contrary to the name of Jesus of Nazareth."],[1089.48,"Now notice how he refers to him."],[1091.24,"He refers to as Jesus of Nazareth."],[1093.08,"Why do you think he refers to as Jesus of Nazareth?"],[1095.72,"That's on it because he's in Galilee."],[1098.92,"Agrippa is in charge of Galilee."],[1101.4,"Uh-huh. So he points out that Jesus was from Nazareth."],[1105.18,"He's one of your people."],[1106.22,"Uh-huh. As a matter of fact, King Agrippa."],[1110.7,"This I also did in Jerusalem, and many of the saints I shut up in prison, having received authority from the chief priests, and when they were put to death, I cast my vote against them."],[1118.46,"So he says, Look, I used to be exactly where they are"],[1125.34,"I I get where they're coming from, because that used to be me."],[1129.03,"I thought Jesus of Nazareth was a false Messiah."],[1131.83,"And when you have a false messiah and followers of a false messiah, they're blasphemers."],[1138.57,"Get rid of them."],[1139.53,"So he says, So I was I was right there."],[1143.69,"And I punished them often in every synagogue and compelled them to blaspheme."],[1150.01,"And being exceedingly enraged against them, I persecuted them even to foreign cities."],[1155.61,"Now notice what he points out, persecuted them to foreign cities."],[1158.49,"That's exactly what they're now doing to him."],[1159.95,"They're following him from place to place."],[1160.83,"Notice also he says, I f he forced them to blaspheme"],[1162.55,"So he says, I was right there with them."],[1167.67,"What does that mean?"],[1168.47,"He was forcing them to deny Jesus as the Messiah, which he now considers blasphemy."],[1177.91,"While thus occupied, as I journeyed to Damascus with authority and commission from the chief priests, at midday, O king, along the road I saw a light from heaven, brighter than the sun, shining around me than those who journeyed with me."],[1194.31,"And we all had fallen to the ground."],[1197.71,"I heard a voice speaking to me and saying in the Hebrew language, Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting me?"],[1204.39,"It is hard for you to kick against the goads."],[1206.15,"Now this is part at least is exactly what we read before"],[1212.76,"How long will you kick against the goads?"],[1214.36,"We talked about when we talked about uh St."],[1217.64,"Paul's uh I keep calling it a conversion too."],[1220.92,"Uh St. Paul's experience, his call to be an apostle on the road to Damascus, this kicking against the goads is you used a goad, it was basically have a long pointy stick to keep an ox moving who was pulling a cart."],[1234.2,"You'd jab him when he stopped."],[1235.98,"And if it was a particularly honri ox, when you stabbed him, rather than getting moving, he'd just try and kick at you."],[1242.31,"He'd try and fight back."],[1244.39,"You're trying to guide him."],[1246.39,"And he's fighting you."],[1247.51,"And so that's the imagery that God uses towards St."],[1249.11,"that. Paul, you know, Christ uses"],[1254.43,"He says, I'm trying to guide you this way."],[1257.61,"And you're fighting me."],[1258.97,"You're fighting against me."],[1263.53,"So I said, Who are you, Lord?"],[1265.45,"And he said, I am Jesus whom you are persecuting."],[1269.85,"But rise and stand on your feet, for I have appeared to you for this purpose, to make you a minister and a witness, both of the things which you have seen and of the things which I will yet reveal to you."],[1281.13,"I will deliver you from the Jewish people as well as from the Gentiles, to whom I now send you, to open their eyes in order to turn them from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan to God, that they may receive forgiveness of sins and an inheritance among those who are sanctified by faith in me."],[1300.05,"Now, this is a lot more than we got in the first telling."],[1303.59,"In terms of what the Gentiles what Jesus told him."],[1307.99,"And sent him to the Gentiles."],[1309.83,"And I think we mentioned at that point the thing most parallel to what happens to St."],[1315.43,"Paul on the road to Damascus is not a conversion, but is the call of a prophet in the Old Testament."],[1323.75,"Like Isaiah, has this vision where he sees God enthroned, and then, you know, who will we send to go and bring this message?"],[1332.81,"Here I am, send me."],[1334.39,"He's commissioned and sent out as a prophet."],[1336.75,"Um, the language. that's used here, and that St"],[1339.95,"Paul will use in his epistle to the Galatians, where he also talks about this event, about being sent to the Gentiles, parallels a lot of the language specifically in Jeremiah."],[1351.79,"In Jeremiah's call, uh, God says that he formed Jeremiah from his mother's womb to be a prophet to the nations."],[1359.15,"See, we translate that differently in English."],[1362.16,"But the word nations and the word gentiles is the same word."],[1366.06,"So we translate it differently."],[1367.5,"We miss that."],[1368.92,"But so this call is being presented as being very similar to this."],[1372.68,"So this is how Saint Paul sees this event is that Jesus set him apart and called him to be a prophet for this particular time."],[1381.48,"And what Jesus is doing at this particular time."],[1381.8,"' He didnt call him to do something."],[1388.56,"Prophets don't do things."],[1390.72,"Prophets come to bear witness to what God is doing at a particular time."],[1395.28,"And you notice that language is here."],[1397.53,"To be a minister and a witness."],[1399.29,"Uh-huh. So Jeremiah is called to be a prophet to witness the destruction of Jerusalem and the setting into exile."],[1406.71,"They're called to bear witness to these things at this time."],[1410.63,"So St. Paul is saying the same thing."],[1412.15,"I was called as a minister and witness to what God is now doing among the Gentiles."],[1416.33,"Which started at Pentecost, we saw before Paul St."],[1421.77,"Paul was called."],[1422.49,"So this was already happening."],[1423.77,"The Samaritans were already converting."],[1424.57,"already. Peter had gone to Cornelius"],[1425.41,"The Ethiopian eunuch had already converted."],[1427.33,"St. So this was already started before St."],[1432.51,"Paul. But Saint Paul is called to be the apostle to the nations in the sense that he's called to be the one to come and bear witness to this act of God."],[1441.07,"As God now brings back."],[1442.59,"Remember, the nations were scattered at Babel."],[1445.81,"There it is."],[1446.69,"And now God is bringing them back."],[1449.17,"And so this major redemptive event is happening in St."],[1452.29,"Paul is called to be the one to uh to be the apostle in this situation."],[1459.09,"They're moving from darkness to light, and notice, from Satan to God."],[1463.57,"And we talked about how in the Tower of Babel story, the rest of the nations were sort of given over and they've been under the control of these demonic powers."],[1472.36,"That for Saint Paul, these pagan gods aren't non existent."],[1476.76,"We're very modern people."],[1478.78,"So we say, oh, Zeus, ha ha ha ha."],[1480.7,"You know, this is a funny story."],[1482.7,"That's not how they viewed it."],[1484.62,"We're gonna see as we go through, we'll see more of this as we go through the New Testament."],[1488.88,"St. Paul is gonna reference the psalm that says all the gods of the nations are demons."],[1493.2,"That's pretty clear."],[1494.98,"He's gonna say what the what the pagans sacrifice, they sacrifice to demons."],[1500.23,"And and so they believe that these are demonic forces, these evil powers that have enslaved them."],[1506.95,"And Saint Paul's gonna write about that."],[1508.47,"He's gonna refer to them as elemental spirits to whom they've been enslaved."],[1511.11,"enslaved. By worshiping these things, they've become to them"],[1514.83,"And now by God reclaiming the nations."],[1517.57,"It's not just like that, well, Israel used to be special and now everybody's special."],[1522.69,"It's not just, well, Israel had the scriptures and now everybody's going to have the scriptures."],[1526.53,"It's, you know, they are now, they're now no longer going to be under the power of these dark forces."],[1534.13,"But are coming back to God."],[1535.63,"Because Christ is now enthroned."],[1538.29,"And he says, all authority in heaven and on earth that' beens given to me."],[1542.37,"And this was this was part of the idea of the Messiah, anyway."],[1547.41,"That the Messiah would would rule not just the Jews, but everybody."],[1552.53,"Everybody. No, I was thinking, I listened to like a podcast sometimes uh Jane Dyer who's talking about um the book of Daniel right and I never really thought about it is that that the the book of Daniel when um the son of man comes and he goes before the ancient days that that is the the uh the ascension right when the son of man in in Daniel 7 yeah and I was I was just thinking is it like when maybe I this is probably not the the place to talk about but when that beast is thrown I know a lot of times like contemporary Christians always think, oh, that's that's the uh the end times."],[1592.55,"That's the beast that he's thrown."],[1594.23,"But is it is it the uh the the beast that has ensnared all the other nations of the earth."],[1599.95,"right All the the pagans and getting destroyed, it's spiritual destruction,?"],[1605.08,"Right not like a uh the estychological, like the the the end of days in the sense of uh the end of the world, you know?"],[1613.0,"Yeah, armies in a big battle between human arms."],[1615.98,"When they say kings, it could be a spiritual kingdom has been destroyed, yeah."],[1620.67,"That's exactly what it is."],[1621.79,"And it in Daniel, when you when you read, you know, when uh when uh the Archangel Gabriel comes to him, he says, Oh, sorry."],[1630.11,"You know, sorry I'm late."],[1631.79,"Sorry I was delayed."],[1632.99,"I was fighting with a Prince of Persia."],[1637.53,"To get here."],[1638.17,"Well, who's that Prince of Persia?"],[1639.69,"Well, it's one of the demonic powers in Persia, and he mentions he, says, next will be the Prince of the Greeks."],[1643.53,"He's not talking about Alexander the Great"],[1646.21,"Uh-huh. Is going to come."],[1649.03,"He's talking about these spiritual, these spiritual powers."],[1654.08,"And remember in St."],[1655.52,"John's gospel, when Christ is getting ready to go to his death, he says, now is the prince of this world judged."],[1663.26,"And that's that's what he's talking about."],[1665.02,"He's talking about, you know, that a big part, this is going to be a major theme in St."],[1668.94,"Paul's Epistles. It's neglected a lot, but it's a major theme in St."],[1671.9,"Paul's epistles. That Christ, through his death and resurrection, defeats these evil spiritual powers."],[1680.56,"He defeats them and destroys them."],[1683.52,"And so, yeah, it's not from the perspective of the ancient Jews of the early Christians."],[1690.62,"They didn't see Caesar as just a man."],[1695.0,"They saw Caesar, yes, Caesar is a man."],[1698.29,"He's a human person."],[1699.41,"But they also saw that there were these dark spiritual powers behind Caesar."],[1705.59,"Who are sort of animating Caesar and motivating Caesar?"],[1708.71,"Or if you had empowering Caesar."],[1711.35,"They were. Gods."],[1712.47,"They were divine and to be worshipped."],[1713.99,"People say the same things about the least today."],[1716.79,"And so they'd worship Caesar."],[1718.69,"As a God."],[1719.39,"Yeah. And so, yeah, that's we we need to we need to remember that because we're also very modern, you know, and oh, Pishposh mythology."],[1727.15,"But when God, after the Exodus, says, Now I have defeated the gods of Egypt, he means he doesn't mean I've defeated some imaginary concepts."],[1735.53,"Or fictional characters."],[1737.29,"He means I have defeated the gods of Egypt."],[1740.34,"And display that they're not gods."],[1743.86,"In the the stories of Christ's infancy in Egypt, the various legends that have come, he destroys the gods of Egypt."],[1755.38,"Yeah, literally. Yeah."],[1756.9,"The statues fall down and it's connected to that idea."],[1761.06,"It's conveying that idea in a dramatic way."],[1763.7,"But so that's what that's what St."],[1764.9,"Paul is getting at when he says, these Gentiles now, through the power of the gospel."],[1770.28,"It's not when they're being reclaimed, it doesn't just mean like, oh now, you can become a Christian if you want to."],[1773.08,"It means something has fundamentally changed in the world"],[1780.46,"And now these judges are being brought out of the power of Satan and under the power of and the blessing of God."],[1787.82,"And this is why we bless things in the church."],[1791.28,"Because these things that are out in the world, they didn't have an idea of secular space in the ancient world."],[1799.44,"We think there's this secular space that's like neutral."],[1802.48,"Uh-huh. In fact, we we those things are there."],[1806.63,"And so for them, it was either under God's power and authority and blessing, or it was outside of that, which meant it was under. the control of the devil and the dark spiritual powers"],[1822.25,"It goes all the way back into the Torah with there's the camp where God is in the tabernacle, then surrounding it."],[1828.41,"Tabernacles smack in the middle."],[1831.05,"And then there's the wilderness outside, which is ruled by who in Leviticus in the Day of Atonement ritual?"],[1837.69,"Azazel. This demonic power, that's out there in the wilderness."],[1843.45,"And it goes all the way back."],[1844.73,"And so the perspective here is that that that sacred space and that holy space is now expanding out."],[1851.29,"And the world's being reclaimed by the church."],[1855.21,"And so this is why we bless water, we bless objects, we bless people, we bless people's houses, we bless you know, livestock, we bless new cars."],[1860.17,"We bless all these things"],[1867.55,"Bringing it into a modern context, would we say that the what we now think of as the secular space is space that's under demonic control."],[1878.83,"There's no such thing as a secular space, yes."],[1882.11,"It's one or the other."],[1883.71,"It's one or the other."],[1884.75,"Nobody's neutral, nobody's unaffiliated."],[1888.76,"And that's what that's why we bless all these things."],[1890.84,"When we bless something, we're not making it magical."],[1894.36,"When we bless something, we're returning it to its original purpose for which God created it."],[1899.0,"So when we bless water, we're returning that water to what God created water for, to refresh us, to strengthen us, to clean clean and cleanse."],[1909.22,"From all the other things that water might have been used for out in the out in the world."],[1915.54,"I think that's really important because we I mean I certainly do slip into this well, there's there's the neutral ordinary world, nothing wrong with it."],[1928.02,"And then there's things that are holy."],[1934.74,"This is uh this is a really dangerous idea from point of view of of Christianity, of an Orthodox Christianity."],[1946.82,"Because it it we drop all our guards."],[1950.62,"Yeah. Yeah. You think about society we consider secularism usually just mammoths."],[1957.9,"Mm-hmm. Yeah. When you look at the God behind us, when you take Christ out of Christmas, what do you what do you have?"],[1963.9,"You know? You have a complete celebration of capitalism."],[1970.06,"Yeah, you you you have Black Friday."],[1973.42,"Suddenly everything's profitable."],[1975.34,"Yeah. Yeah. And and that, you know, that form of Christmas is spread around the world, it's celebrated in Japan and China."],[1983.42,"Yeah. Every place."],[1984.7,"Because merchants like to make money."],[1987.18,"And because we've we've also lied to ourselves and convinced ourselves that we're a lot more sophisticated than we actually are."],[1995.48,"So we laugh, we would laugh at ancient pagans for worshiping like Venus, a goddess of beauty, and Mars, a god of war, and Mammon as an actual god, or Pluto as a god of wealth."],[2008.28,"You're not going to convince me that people in this country don't worship beauty, wealth, war, victory."],[2017.16,"You know? Don't worship these things."],[2020.04,"And I can prove that they worship these things because they sacrifice things to them."],[2025.64,"And if you sacrifice things to them, you're worshiping them."],[2029.16,"The fact that we don't personify them does not make us any more sophisticated."],[2033.96,"It just makes us less aware of what we're actually doing than the pagans were."],[2041.52,"Yeah. And so this is this is how this is how St."],[2046.64,"Paul sees what he's doing and what is happening in the world."],[2049.68,"That the world's being reclaimed."],[2052.0,"Not just the people out of the world, but the world, the creation itself is now being reclaimed by God."],[2060.64,"Who didn't abandon, it alienated itself from him."],[2063.92,"But he's now reclaiming it and taking it back."],[2069.12,"As he said in Acts 17 in Athens, he said, For a time God allowed the nations to wander."],[2075.36,"But now he is calling them all back through repentance and faith, which is what St."],[2081.68,"Paul says here."],[2085.88,"Therefore, King Agrippa, I was not disobedient to the heavenly vision, but desired, but declared first to those in Damascus and in Jerusalem and throughout all the region of Judea, and then to the Gentiles, that they should repent, turn to God, and do works befitting repentance."],[2106.04,"So he says, This is what I did."],[2107.96,"I did what the vision I received from God told me to do."],[2112.62,"Which that's hard to argue with if you're actually a Jewish person."],[2116.14,"If you receive a vision of God and he tells you to do something, you should do it."],[2120.86,"But he also says, This is what I preach."],[2122.78,"I'm preaching to all of these people."],[2124.7,"Both the Jewish people I started preaching to, and then the Gentiles to everyone that they need to repent, turn to God, meaning the God of Israel, and do works of repentance."],[2139.38,"For these reasons, the Jews seized me in the temple and tried to kill me."],[2144.6,"So how does that make sense?"],[2146.81,"Me going around telling people to repent."],[2149.19,"They're mad that I'm associating with these Gentiles."],[2151.99,"Well, I'm telling them to repent of their idolatry and of their immorality."],[2161.03,"Therefore, having obtained help from God, to this day I stand witnessing both to small and great, saying no other thing than those which the prophets and Moses said would come."],[2172.19,"That the Christ would suffer, that he would be the first to rise from the dead, and would proclaim light to the Jewish people and to the Gentiles."],[2182.99,"And this is a quick summary."],[2185.87,"We'll loop back around when we get to the end of the New Testament and the Old Testament, but this is a pretty quick summary of what's in the prophets."],[2193.71,"In terms of what they say."],[2207.55,"That he would be the first to rise from the dead, not the last, not the only, but the first."],[2212.79,"He'd signal the coming of the Messiah would signal the beginning of the resurrection of the dead, and would proclaim light to the Jewish people and to the Gentiles."],[2220.95,"And that's all through."],[2222.65,"That the the when the Messiah comes, it's not only going to be for the Jews, but it's going to be for all the nations."],[2227.61,"The Psalm says, I'll have an altar where incense is burned in Egypt."],[2231.13,"I'll have. God says that throughout the whole world."],[2235.23,"So he says, and this is all I'm preaching to everybody."],[2238.59,"I'm preaching what the Torah and the prophets teach."],[2242.59,"I'm teaching people to repent."],[2244.59,"What could possibly be the problem?"],[2249.79,"Now, as he thus made his defense, Festus said with a loud voice, Paul, you are beside yourself."],[2256.11,"Much learning is driving you mad."],[2259.39,"Which is how I feel when I'm working on my dissertation."],[2262.63,"But aside from that."],[2267.33,"Festus clearly still doesn't get what he's talking about."],[2270.82,"Because of course he's never read a Torah or a prophet in his life."],[2274.92,"So he's just like, what is all this nonsense?"],[2277.4,"What is this babble?"],[2279.37,"He's like, clearly, because this is really good rhetorically."],[2282.19,"Rhetorical style. He could tell this is an educated guy, but what is this not, you know."],[2293.71,"But he said, I am not mad, most noble Festus, but speak the words of truth and reason."],[2301.15,"For the king before whom I also speak freely knows these things, for I am convinced that none of these things escapes his attention, since this thing was not done in a corner."],[2311.99,"King Agrippa, do you believe the prophets?"],[2314.63,"I know that you do believe."],[2317.43,"So he says, No, tr no, really."],[2320.09,"What I'm saying is truth."],[2322.01,"What I'm saying makes sense."],[2324.58,"He says, and I know it makes sense to Agrippa."],[2327.6,"It may not make sense to you, but I know Agrippa knows exactly what I'm talking about."],[2331.5,"He says, You believe what the prophets teach."],[2333.44,"Oh, I know you do."],[2336.0,"So you must agree with me, Agrippa."],[2338.9,"Then Agrippa said to Paul, You almost persuade me to become a Christian."],[2343.94,"Christian. So, by the fact that he uses the word, he does St"],[2351.9,"Paul's right, he does know."],[2353.26,"Uh huh. He does know what this is all about on some level."],[2357.26,"Uh-huh. On some level."],[2359.11,"He said, You almost."],[2361.27,"Not quite. But you almost."],[2365.43,"And Paul said, I would to God that not only you, but also all who hear me today, might become both almost and altogether such as I am, except for these chains."],[2380.61,"I don't wish you were a prisoner like me, but I do that."],[2383.73,"I wish you would all come to see what I've come to see."],[2389.45,"When he had said these things, the king stood up as well as the governor and Bernice and those who sat with them."],[2397.21,"And when they had gone aside, they talked among themselves, saying, This man is doing nothing deserving of death or chains."],[2404.59,"It's like over time, it's like I got nothing."],[2408.11,"In terms of a charge."],[2408.99,"Because remember, the goal here was to come up with something to write as terms of a charge against him when he gets packed on to Caesar."],[2415.49,"It's like and he this is."],[2421.41,"So I don't know what to say."],[2422.77,"But it's still not breaking any of the laws."],[2426.29,"Yeah. It's not against the law to be a crazy person."],[2428.93,"Yeah. And have a weird religion."],[2430.79,"Uh-huh. Then Agrippa said to Festus, this might have been, this man might have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar."],[2440.93,"So he's like, yeah, because I mean, now that he appealed to Caesar, you've got to send him to Rome, but I I have no idea what to say in terms of why."],[2452.34,"So we'll go ahead and and uh stop here for tonight."],[2457.46,"I don't want to make too bold predictions."],[2460.34,"You probably have two Bible studies left in Acts, but it might only be one."],[2464.58,"But um there's only two chapters left."],[2468.9,"So we'll see."],[2469.86,"This is this is where it gets uh exciting."],[2472.98,"It's another campaign."],[2474.34,"There's gonna be a ship."],[2475.34,"There's gonna be a shipwreck."],[2476.66,"There's gonna be adventures at sea"],[2477.42,"That's right. Um so thank you, everybody."],[2484.62,"Thank you. Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2493.82,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2499.18,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.32,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 26:1-3 - Paul begins his defense","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Agrippa: <b>\"you are permitted to speak for yourself\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>stretched out his hand</b> - odd phrase; apparently spoke with his hands (\"despite not being the Italian in the room\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Opens: <b>\"I think myself happy, King Agrippa\"</b> to answer before you</li><li class=\"d1\">\"expert in all customs and questions which have to do with the Jews\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I beg you to hear me patiently\"</li></ul>"},{"t":95.64,"lv":3,"h":"Public oratory and rhetoric","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">No TV, radio, internet; most people <b>illiterate</b>, couldn't afford books</li><li class=\"d1\">hearing public orations, debates, discussions = <b>part of daily life</b>, not just synagogue sermons</li><li class=\"d1\">cf. <b>Athens</b> - Paul joining the marketplace discussions</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: scriptures with <b>rabbinical training</b> + <b>classical Roman education</b></li><li class=\"d1\">quotes <b>Stoic philosophers</b> in epistles</li><li class=\"d1\">learned speaking and <b>methods of rhetoric</b></li><li class=\"d0\">To us it seems like <b>sucking up</b> - standard opening: flatter the audience</li><li class=\"d1\">rhetoric creates a <b>relationship</b> between speaker and audience; compliment them, hope they compliment you by the end</li><li class=\"d0\">Also a point: Agrippa, unlike <b>Festus</b> and <b>Felix</b> (married to a Jewish woman), understands <b>Jewish culture</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I can speak to you on a different level\" - you'll get where I'm coming from</li></ul>"},{"t":263.21,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 26:4-5 - Strictest sect: a Pharisee","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"All the Jews know\"</b> my life from the first - <b>strictest sect</b>, a <b>Pharisee</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a stranger who wandered into Jerusalem and caused trouble</li><li class=\"d1\">at the start of Acts he was <b>working for them</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pharisees: if nothing else, <b>strict</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>hedge around the law</b>: name of the Lord in vain → never say or write the name at all</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Sabbath</b> rules: what is work? only walk so far, detailed rules</li><li class=\"d1\">Orthodox Judaism keeps it: can't tear toilet paper; no light switch (<b>spark = kindling fire</b>); <b>pneumatic elevators</b> in New York</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem with Christ: they <b>confuse the extra rules with the sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">walk > <b>quarter mile</b> = violated Sabbath, worthy of death</li><li class=\"d1\">healing on the Sabbath = \"work\"; Jesus: <b>\"lawful to do good or evil on the Sabbath?\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">made rules part of God's law - things <b>He never said</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Agrippa would know Pharisees are strict; they'd avoid him - a Gentile; <b>hated the Herodians</b></li></ul>"},{"t":467.47,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 26:6-8 - Hope of the resurrection","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Judged for the hope of the promise made by God to our fathers\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>twelve tribes</b> serving God night and day hope to attain it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Why should it be thought incredible... that God raises the dead?\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same move as before the <b>Sanhedrin</b>: Pharisees vs. Sadducees, Pharisees took his side</li><li class=\"d0\">Pharisee view: on the <b>last day</b> God visits the world, resurrection - not (yet) Christ risen</li><li class=\"d1\">seen in <b>2 Maccabees</b>, <b>Wisdom</b> - late OT period</li><li class=\"d1\">God promised <b>justice</b>; yet righteous suffer, wicked prosper → resurrection is the answer</li><li class=\"d1\">righteous get reward; wicked prosperers get <b>punishment</b></li></ul>"},{"t":601.42,"lv":3,"h":"Aside: development of the resurrection hope","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>Ezekiel's dry bones</b> point that way? - develops through the whole testament</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 Maccabees</b>: martyrs tortured to death - \"God will <b>knit my body back together</b> on the last day\" - very literal</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Job</b> has references</li><li class=\"d0\">Like the <b>Messiah</b>: in <b>Genesis 3</b>, seed of the woman → clearer and clearer until Christ's birth</li><li class=\"d1\">revealed and understood <b>gradually</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Jews today? Same as then: <b>Sadducees</b> didn't, Pharisees did</li><li class=\"d1\">some Jews believe in afterlife, some don't; mostly <b>Orthodox</b> do</li><li class=\"d1\">Reform: some don't even believe God exists - just tradition and way of life</li><li class=\"d1\">some Orthodox sects don't either; Conservative = in between</li></ul>"},{"t":741.14,"lv":3,"h":"Promise, judgment, and the Pharisees","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Promise to the fathers</b> = blessings and curses at end of <b>Deuteronomy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We think of judgment as negative - largely right, we're <b>sinners</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also we're more often on side of the <b>well-to-do and oppressor</b> than the oppressed</li><li class=\"d1\">God vindicating <b>orphans and widows</b>, starving - not most of us</li><li class=\"d0\">Pharisees sure they're on the righteous side - blessed, enslave \"rotten Gentiles\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus: you're not on the side you think - <b>Pharisee and the publican</b>; repentant tax collector is righteous</li><li class=\"d0\">Agrippa wouldn't hear \"Jesus rose\" - just: why weird that <b>God could raise the dead</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul starting at <b>Pharisee level</b> of resurrection, to bring him on board; going elsewhere next</li></ul>"},{"t":866.39,"lv":3,"h":"Paul didn't change religions","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We say <b>\"conversion\"</b>: Jew/Pharisee → Christian</li><li class=\"d1\">happened ~<b>35 AD</b>, 2-3 years after the Resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">no two religions yet to leave/join - Jews who accepted Jesus as Messiah, and those who hadn't (rejected or hadn't heard)</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: did Paul see a difference? <b>Israel = the church</b>; a <b>remnant</b> accepted Jesus; Gentiles <b>grafted in</b> (Romans); those who didn't cut themselves off</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul calls himself a <b>Pharisee, present tense</b>, several times in epistles</li><li class=\"d1\">same beliefs: Messiah coming, <b>resurrection</b>, Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">what changed: <b>where he is in the timeline of redemption</b> - Messiah is coming → <b>has come</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Keeps saying: <b>I haven't abandoned</b> Jewish faith or Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">ultimately argues in Romans <b>they</b> abandoned it, he stayed true</li><li class=\"d0\">Starts at common Pharisee level Agrippa has heard - <b>a starting point</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1080.28,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 26:9-11 - Paul the persecutor","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Contrary to the name of Jesus of Nazareth\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Q: why Nazareth? Agrippa rules <b>Galilee</b> - one of your people</li><li class=\"d0\">Locked up many saints, authority from chief priests; <b>cast my vote</b> against them when put to death</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I used to be exactly where they are\" - Jesus a <b>false Messiah</b>, followers blasphemers, get rid of them</li><li class=\"d0\">Punished them in every synagogue, <b>compelled them to blaspheme</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= forced them to <b>deny Jesus as Messiah</b>, which he now calls blasphemy</li><li class=\"d0\">Persecuted them to <b>foreign cities</b> - exactly what they now do to him</li></ul>"},{"t":1177.91,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 26:12-14 - Damascus road; goads","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Midday, light from heaven <b>brighter than the sun</b>; all fell to the ground</li><li class=\"d0\">Voice <b>in the Hebrew language</b>: \"Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting me?\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Hard for you to kick against the goads\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">goad = long pointy stick to keep an <b>ox pulling a cart</b> moving</li><li class=\"d1\">stubborn ox kicks back at you</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: I'm guiding you this way and you're <b>fighting me</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1263.53,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 26:15-18 - The commission","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Who are you, Lord?\"</b> - \"I am Jesus whom you are persecuting\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Rise and stand... to make you a <b>minister and a witness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Delivered from Jews and Gentiles; sent <b>to open their eyes</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>darkness → light</b>; <b>power of Satan → God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">forgiveness of sins, <b>inheritance among the sanctified</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Far more than first telling about what Jesus told him re <b>Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Closest parallel = not conversion but <b>call of an OT prophet</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Isaiah</b>: vision of God enthroned, \"Here I am, send me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">language here and in <b>Galatians</b> parallels <b>Jeremiah's call</b> - formed from womb, prophet to the nations</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>nations</i> and <i>Gentiles</i> = same word - English hides it</li></ul>"},{"t":1381.48,"lv":3,"h":"Prophets witness what God is doing","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Prophet doesn't do things - <b>bears witness to what God is doing</b> at a time</li><li class=\"d1\">\"minister and witness\": Jeremiah witnesses <b>destruction of Jerusalem</b> and exile</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: witness to what God is <b>now doing among the Gentiles</b> - began at <b>Pentecost</b></li><li class=\"d1\">already: <b>Samaritans</b>, <b>Peter to Cornelius</b>, the <b>Ethiopian eunuch</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul called to be <b>apostle to the nations</b> - the one to bear witness to this act</li><li class=\"d1\">nations <b>scattered at Babel</b> → God now bringing them back</li><li class=\"d1\">major redemptive event</li></ul>"},{"t":1459.09,"lv":2,"h":"Darkness to light: from Satan to God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">After Babel nations given over to <b>demonic powers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for Paul pagan gods aren't non-existent - \"we're very modern people\": Zeus, ha ha</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul cites the psalm: <b>all the gods of the nations are demons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">pagan sacrifice = <b>sacrifice to demons</b>; <b>elemental spirits</b> they're enslaved to (Galatians)</li><li class=\"d0\">God reclaiming nations: not just \"Israel was special, now everyone is\", not just everybody gets the scriptures</li><li class=\"d1\">no longer under <b>dark forces</b> - Christ <b>enthroned</b>: <b>\"all authority in heaven and on earth\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">always the idea of the <b>Messiah</b>: rule everybody</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>Daniel 7</b> - Son of Man before the Ancient of Days = <b>Ascension</b>? beast thrown = spiritual destruction, not end-times armies? A: <b>exactly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Daniel 10: Gabriel \"sorry I'm late\" - fighting the <b>Prince of Persia</b>; next <b>Prince of the Greeks</b> = demonic powers, not Alexander</li><li class=\"d1\">John: <b>\"now is the prince of this world judged\"</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1671.9,"lv":3,"h":"Christ defeats the powers; gods are real","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Major, neglected theme in Paul: Christ through death and resurrection <b>defeats and destroys</b> evil spiritual powers</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancients/early Christians: <b>Caesar</b> a man, but dark powers behind him animating him - worshiped as a god</li><li class=\"d1\">same said of leaders today</li><li class=\"d0\">Exodus: <b>\"I have defeated the gods of Egypt\"</b> - not imaginary concepts or fictional characters</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ's infancy in Egypt legends: <b>statues fall down</b> - conveys the idea dramatically</li><li class=\"d0\">Gentiles reclaimed ≠ \"now you can become a Christian if you want\" - <b>something fundamentally changed in the world</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1787.82,"lv":2,"h":"No secular space - why we bless things","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why we <b>bless things</b> in the church</li><li class=\"d1\">ancients had no idea of <b>secular, neutral space</b></li><li class=\"d1\">either under <b>God's blessing</b> or outside it = control of the devil and dark powers</li><li class=\"d0\">Back in Torah: camp with <b>tabernacle</b> in the middle; <b>wilderness</b> outside ruled by <b>Azazel</b> (Day of Atonement)</li><li class=\"d0\">Holy space <b>expanding out</b>; world reclaimed by the church</li><li class=\"d1\">bless water, objects, people, houses, livestock, <b>new cars</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: secular space = under demonic control? <b>No such thing</b> - \"nobody's neutral, nobody's unaffiliated\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Blessing isn't making it magical - <b>returning it to its original purpose</b></li><li class=\"d1\">water: refresh, strengthen, <b>cleanse</b> - from other uses in the world</li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: \"neutral ordinary world\" vs. holy = <b>dangerous idea</b> - \"we drop all our guards\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1950.62,"lv":3,"h":"Modern idols: Christmas, Mammon, Venus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Secularism: take Christ out of Christmas → <b>celebration of capitalism</b>, <b>Black Friday</b></li><li class=\"d1\">spread to Japan and China - \"merchants like to make money\"</li><li class=\"d0\">We laugh at pagans worshiping <b>Venus</b> (beauty), <b>Mars</b> (war), <b>Mammon</b>, <b>Pluto</b> (wealth)</li><li class=\"d1\">people here worship <b>beauty, wealth, war, victory</b> - \"I can prove it: they <b>sacrifice things</b> to them\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not personifying them doesn't make us more sophisticated - <b>less aware</b> of what we're doing</li></ul>"},{"t":2041.52,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 26:19-20 - Obedient to the vision","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">World being reclaimed - <b>creation itself</b>, not just people out of it</li><li class=\"d1\">it alienated itself; God not abandoning</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Acts 17</b>: for a time God let nations wander; now calls all back through <b>repentance and faith</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Not disobedient to the heavenly vision\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">declared first at <b>Damascus, Jerusalem, Judea</b>, then Gentiles: <b>repent, turn to God, works befitting repentance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hard to argue with for a Jew: a vision from God → do it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>turn to God</b> = the God of Israel</li></ul>"},{"t":2139.38,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 26:21-23 - Moses and the prophets","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"For these reasons the Jews seized me in the temple\"</b> - how does telling people to repent make sense?</li><li class=\"d1\">really mad he associates with Gentiles; he tells them to repent of <b>idolatry and immorality</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Witnessing to small and great\" saying nothing but what <b>the prophets and Moses</b> said would come</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ would <b>suffer</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>first to rise from the dead</b> - not last, not only, <b>first</b>; begins the resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">proclaim <b>light to the Jews and Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Quick summary of the prophets - will loop back at end of NT and OT</li><li class=\"d0\">Messiah not only for Jews but <b>all nations</b>: Psalm - altar with incense in <b>Egypt</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"What could possibly be the problem?\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2249.79,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 26:24-29 - Festus: \"You are mad\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Festus loudly: <b>\"Paul, you are beside yourself. Much learning is driving you mad\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"how I feel working on my dissertation\"</li><li class=\"d1\">never read Torah or a prophet - \"what is this babble?\"; recognizes good rhetoric, an educated man</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>\"I am not mad, most noble Festus\"</b> - words of truth and reason</li><li class=\"d1\">Agrippa knows these things - <b>\"not done in a corner\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"King Agrippa, do you believe the prophets? I know that you do\"</b> - so you must agree with me</li><li class=\"d0\">Agrippa: <b>\"You almost persuade me to become a Christian\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he uses the word - knows on some level what it's about; <b>almost, not quite</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: wish <b>all</b> who hear me were <b>almost and altogether</b> such as I am - <b>except these chains</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not wishing you were a prisoner - wish you'd see what I've seen</li></ul>"},{"t":2389.45,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 26:30-32 - Verdict: not deserving of death","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">King, governor, Bernice rise; aside they say: <b>\"doing nothing deserving of death or chains\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">still no charge to write for Caesar - \"not against the law to be a crazy person and have a weird religion\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Agrippa: <b>\"might have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">now must send him to Rome - but no idea what to say why</li></ul>"}],"next":"Probably <b>two</b> more Bible studies left in Acts, maybe one - <b>two chapters</b> left (27-28); next: another voyage, a <b>ship</b>, a <b>shipwreck</b>, adventures at sea"});
